    "datePosted": "2026-11-17T13:13:18.000Z",
    "description": "Position Title: English Language Learner (ELL) Support Teacher Department: Department of Learning & Development (DoLD) Reports To: Learning Support Coordinator Employment Type: Full-Time POSITION PURPOSE The ELL Support Teacher serves students in iPrimary and iLower Secondary who are learning English as an additional language, helping them to access the full LionHeart curriculum and to take part fully in school life. The role includes assessing students' English proficiency, planning and delivering targeted language support, and coaching classroom teachers to make lessons accessible to all learners. Where time allows, the role may also support English Language Arts (ELA) teaching and literacy intervention. The successful candidate will work closely with classroom teachers, the Special Education Needs (SEN) Department, academic leaders, and families to ensure that every student can think, speak, read, and write in English with confidence, while their home languages and cultures are valued as strengths. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES A. Assessment, Identification & Placement Administer and interpret English proficiency assessments for new and current students, using a recognized framework. Maintain an up-to-date ELL register recording each student's language background, proficiency level, goals, and support plan. Recommend support levels and placement in collaboration with the Department of Learning and Development (DoLD), SEN Department, and classroom teachers. Distinguish language needs from learning needs, and refer students to the SEN Department where appropriate. Take part in admissions and parent meetings regarding language support when requested. B. Instruction & Direct Language Support Plan and teach small-group and individual language lessons, using in-class (push-in) and out-of-class (pull-out) models according to student need. Teach academic language across the curriculum: speaking, listening, reading, writing, and subject vocabulary linked to the Pearson units students are studying. Use active, communicative, and play-based approaches such as discussion, role play, games, and projects, in line with LionHeart's approach to active learning. Differentiate tasks and materials by proficiency level, and set clear, measurable language goals for each student. Build students' confidence and independence as English users. C. Collaboration & Teacher Support Co-plan and, where possible, co-teach with classroom teachers so that language support is part of everyday lessons. Coach teachers in practical strategies for English learners, including scaffolding, visuals, sentence frames, vocabulary pre-teaching, and checking for understanding. Share each student's language profile and recommended strategies with the teachers who work with them. Help teachers adapt Pearson materials and assessments so that language does not hide what students know. Work with the SEN Department to keep an inclusive environment, avoiding grouping students apart without a clear learning reason. D. Progress Monitoring & Reporting Track student progress against language goals and reassess at agreed points each term. Contribute comments on language development to student reports. Review support plans each term, including when a student is ready to reduce or exit support. Maintain accurate, confidential records and provide a brief summary report to DoLD leadership each term. E. Parent & Community Engagement Communicate regularly and respectfully with families about their child's English progress, using clear and simple language. Offer families practical ideas for supporting English and home-language development at home. Take part in parent conferences, information sessions, and school events. F. School Culture, Safeguarding & Professional Growth Model the LionHeart culture: first names used consistently, warm relationships with students, and genuine enthusiasm for students and colleagues. Apply the school's behavior approach of consequences rather than punishment, and asking rather than telling. Follow all safeguarding and child-protection procedures and physical-boundary expectations, and report concerns immediately. Set yearly professional goals with the Principal, and take part in professional development, peer observation, and professional learning communities. Welcome observation and feedback on lesson plans and teaching. G. English Language Arts (ELA) Support (as time allows) Where the ELL caseload and schedule allow, support English Language Arts teaching in iPrimary and iLower Secondary, as agreed with DoLD leadership. Work with ELA teachers to plan literacy support for students who need extra help with reading, writing, speaking, and listening. Deliver small-group literacy or ELA intervention sessions, and help teachers adapt ELA tasks and assessments. Contribute to monitoring literacy progress and recommending next steps for students. Give priority to ELL responsibilities if time is limited. H. Other Duties Support special projects assigned by the Department of Learning & Development. Perform other duties reasonably assigned by school leadership.",
