    "description": "Call for Expressions of Interest: Members of CECOE's Electoral Reform Technical Reference Group (TRG) 1. About CECOE The Coalition of Ethiopian Civil Society Organizations for Elections (CECOE) is a non-profit, non-governmental network of over 190 member civil society organisations. It was licensed by the Authority for Civil Society Organisations (ACSO) in 2019 (registration no. 4748) under Proclamation 1113/2019, and coordinates Ethiopian CSOs in election observation, voter education, preventing electoral conflict and capacity building. CECOE is a member of E-HORN, AfEONet and the Global Network of Domestic Election Monitors (GNDEM). Since its establishment, CECOE has observed the Sidama referendum, the 6th General Election, the South West and South Ethiopia referendums, the Wolaita Sodo referendum rerun, the outstanding and re-elections in Benishangul Gumuz, Afar, Somali , and Meskan and Mareko, and the 7th General Election, deploying 8,119 observers. 2. Background The Coalition of Ethiopian Civil Society Organisations for Elections (CECOE), with the support of the Embassy of Ireland, is implementing the 24-month project \"Strengthening Civic Education and Evidence-Based Electoral Reform Advocacy\", aimed at strengthening democratic participation and electoral integrity across Ethiopia through practical continuous civic education, organised community civic action and evidence based advocacy for meaningful electoral reform. One of the pillars of this project is generating evidence based advocacy campaigns. Through this project CECOE seeks to secure the institutional adoption of critical electoral reforms, the engagement of electoral institutions, legislative bodies and political parties with evidence based reform proposals. 3. Rationale To ensure its research outputs are methodologically rigorous, aligned with key reform priorities, and highly credible prior to publication, CECOE is establishing the Electoral Reform Technical Reference Group (TRG) to provide expert, independent oversight. CECOE welcomes applications from qualified specialists to join the TRG. To ensure full representation across required technical domains and maintain background diversity, CECOE may also directly contact targeted experts, who will undergo the identical screening process as standard applicants. 4. Role of the Technical Reference Group The TRG will: Assess the Secretariat's progress against the agreed research agenda and workplan. Review, clear and approve draft study terms of reference, methodologies and data collection instruments before fieldwork begins. Review draft research reports and give written technical feedback before they are submitted for public validation. Advise CECOE on how research findings can inform its electoral reform advocacy and engagement with policymakers. CECOE serves as the Secretariat to the TRG. 5. Composition of the Technical Reference Group The TRG will have nine members, one from each of the following fields: Electoral law Election administration (operations and logistics) (*2) Gender and social inclusion Disability inclusion and rights Political party and media regulation National dialogue / ECSOC Academia and research Civil society working on elections CECOE will aim for gender balance and the inclusion of persons with disabilities across the group. 6. Criteria for Membership Candidates must have: Academic background: at least a master's degree in a field relevant to one of the areas above. Experience: at least seven years of relevant professional or research experience. Knowledge: sound knowledge of Ethiopia's electoral, legal and institutional framework. Non-partisanship: no current leadership role in a political party, no candidacy for public office. Integrity: a proven record of professional ethics and independent judgement. Availability: time to attend meetings and review documents within agreed timelines. Members serve in their individual technical capacity, not as representatives of their institutions. Each member signs a conflict-of-interest declaration and a confidentiality undertaking before taking up the role.",
